## Naming Conventions

| Type | Convention | Examples |
|------|-----------|----------|
| C# classes/methods/properties | `PascalCase` | `GameState`, `TryMove()`, `CurrentLocation` |
| C# local variables | `camelCase` | `edgesInDirection`, `isLocked` |
| C# private fields | `_camelCase` | `_instance`, `_items`, `_currentLanguage` |
| Game IDs (dungeons, locations, edges) | `snake_case` | `test_dungeon`, `entrance`, `corridor_to_locked_room` |
| Localization keys | `dot.separated.snake_case` | `location.entrance.name`, `ui.menu.new_game` |
| GameState flags/variables | `snake_case` | `treasure_room_unlocked`, `main_quest_stage` |

## Markdown Documentation Rules

**NEVER use inline links** `[text](url)` in markdown files. 
**ALWAYS use reference-style links** `[text][ref-id]` with definitions at the top of the file.

Example:
```markdown
# Document Title

[link-id]: path/to/file.md "Optional Tooltip"
[another-link]: https://example.com

See [this document][link-id] for details.
```

## Error Handling Pattern

```csharp
using FluentResults;

// ✅ Good: Use Result<T> for business logic failures
public Result<Item> RemoveItem(string itemId, int count)
{
    if (!_items.ContainsKey(itemId))
        return Result.Fail<Item>($"Item '{itemId}' not found");
    
    if (_items[itemId].Count < count)
        return Result.Fail<Item>($"Insufficient quantity");
    
    _items[itemId].Count -= count;
    return Result.Ok(_items[itemId]);
}

// Usage
var result = inventoryService.RemoveItem("key", 1);
if (result.IsSuccess)
{
    // Use result.Value
}
else
{
    GD.PrintErr($"Error: {result.Errors[0].Message}");
}
```

## MediatR Pattern

```csharp
using MediatR;

// Query (read data)
public record GetLocationQuery(string LocationId) : IRequest<Result<Location>>;

// Command (modify state)
public record UnlockDoorCommand(string EdgeId) : IRequest<Result>;

// Notification (event)
public record LocationChangedNotification(string LocationId) : INotification;

// Handler
public class GetLocationHandler : IRequestHandler<GetLocationQuery, Result<Location>>
{
    public async Task<Result<Location>> Handle(GetLocationQuery request, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        // Implementation
    }
}
```

## Build & Test Commands

```bash
# Build solution
dotnet build src/Fellhaven.slnx

# Run tests
cd tests
dotnet test

# Run specific test
dotnet test --filter "FullyQualifiedName~DirectionTests"
```

## Commit Message Format

Use Conventional Commits:
```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body>
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`

Example:
```
feat(combat): add critical hit system

- Implemented roll mechanics
- Added critical multiplier to weapons
- Updated combat tests
```

## When Adding New Features

1. Check if it should be data-driven (JSON/Lua) vs hardcoded (C#)
2. Follow SOLID principles - single responsibility
3. Return `Result<T>` for operations that can fail
4. Use MediatR for cross-component communication
5. Add XML documentation to public APIs
6. Write unit tests
7. Update relevant documentation (ADRs, API docs)

## Godot-Specific Patterns

```csharp
// Singleton pattern for Godot Nodes
public partial class GameState : Node
{
    private static GameState? _instance;
    public static GameState Instance => _instance!;
    
    public override void _Ready()
    {
        _instance = this;
    }
}

// Use GD.Print for logging, not Console.WriteLine
GD.Print("Information message");
GD.PrintErr("Error message");
```

## File Organization

```
src/Fellhaven/scripts/
├─ core/        # Game logic: Location, Edge, Direction, GameState, LuaScriptEngine
└─ systems/     # Data loading & services: DungeonLoader, LocalizationManager
```

---

**Remember**: The dungeon graph is data-driven. When adding features, prefer extending the Lua API over adding C# classes. Keep gameplay logic in JSON + Lua for easy iteration without recompilation.

For complete details, consult AGENTS.md, CODING_GUIDELINES.md, and the docs/ folder.
